    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1007/s13385-026-00446-x",
    "abstract": "Abstract Climate change brings serious challenges for various sectors of the economy, including the insurance industry. One important impact is its influence on mortality rates, which directly affect the financial reserves of life insurers. Accurately assessing this relationship is essential for ensuring the sustainability and solvency of life insurance portfolios. In this study, we present a framework for incorporating climate risk into the stress testing of life insurers\u2019 reserves. Using temperature projections generated from a stochastic version of a widely used cost\u2013benefit integrated assessment model, we simulate shocks to mortality rates driven by changes in global average temperatures, and evaluate their impact on pricing and reserving for life insurance and life annuity policies. Our numerical analysis demonstrates material risks of mispricing premiums when climate risk is ignored, and reveals substantial differences in reserve requirements, thereby highlighting the importance of integrating climate change considerations into actuarial modeling and financial planning for life insurers.",
    "field": "Actuarial climate risk",
    "problem": "Traditional actuarial models for life insurance assume stable mortality rates, failing to incorporate the uncertain impact of climate change, which leads to material risks of mispricing premiums and underestimating reserve requirements.",
    "method": "We propose a framework to incorporate climate risk into the stress testing of life insurance reserves by simulating mortality rate shocks using stochastic temperature projections generated from a cost-benefit integrated assessment model.",
    "task": "The task is to present a framework for stress testing life insurance reserves by simulating mortality shocks driven by stochastic temperature projections from a climate-economy model and evaluating their impact on premiums and reserves.",
    "model": [
        "DICE",
        "RICE"
    ],
