            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Gompertz mortality model",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "To quantify the influence of rising temperatures on mortality, we adopt a baseline Gompertz mortality model calibrated to data from the G12 countries, following the methodology of Arandjelovi\u0107 et al. [1].",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Stochastic DICE model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "climate-mortality damage function of Bressler",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "In this paper, to study the impacts of climate change on mortality rates and reserves of life insurers, to which end we use temperature trajectories generated from a stochastic version of the DICE model with all five main uncertainties as identified in Nordhaus [18]. The stochastic DICE model is solved as the decision under uncertainty problem using the deep LSMC method as presented in Arandjelovi\u0107 et al. [2]. To translate the temperature trajectories into temperature-related shock scenarios for mortality rates, we use the climate-mortality damage function of Bressler [4].",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": false
        },
